@divider id="b66" num="X" kind="PART ONE · THE LIBRARY" title="The Sixty-Six/Books" sub="One entry per book, in canonical order, each answering the same six questions so the whole library can be read side by side" foot="X · THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS"

@essay rh="LEARNING THE BIBLE · THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · HOW TO USE THEM"
# The sixty-six books
drop: One entry per book, in canonical order, and every entry answers the same six questions so you can compare across the whole library instead of reading each book alone. None of them replaces the book. They're orientation, enough to know what you're walking into, what the book argues about and which of its pressures the novel can use.
Two of the six questions belong to you more than to the Bible. *What it gives TKC* is where the raw material gets named. *What Dominion would do* is where you decide how the Empire has already processed the book before any character opens it. Answer those in pencil, because they'll change as the manuscript changes.
The narrative books get a full page each, since story is where characters come from and you'll keep coming back to them. The shorter prophetic books and the letters share pages, because what you need from them is a fast, reliable sense of the argument more than a scene inventory. The layout tells you how much material is in there.
k: The six questions
- **Where we are.** The political and historical position.
- **What happens.** The shape of the thing, briefly.
- **Who matters.** The names worth knowing.
- **What it wrestles with.** The internal argument.
- **What it gives TKC.** Usable material.
- **What Dominion would do.** The Empire's use of it.
pull: "Sixty-six books, and none of them was written for you."
side:
k: A reading order, if you want one
stext: Canonical order isn't chronological, and it's a poor first path. To read for competence before completeness, take Genesis, Exodus, Judges, 1–2 Samuel, Mark, Luke, Amos, Isaiah, Esther, Ruth, Romans and Revelation. You'll have origins, law, monarchy, prophetic anger, exile, a life, a movement and an apocalypse, the whole grammar Dominion is built on, in about a tenth of the pages. Come back afterward and do it properly.
marg
see: Bible 101, I to IX; the focused studies and the deep dives that follow this section.
/end

@essay rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · OLD TESTAMENT · TORAH · I"
# Genesis
small: *Bereshit* · fifty chapters · Book i of lxvi
k: Where we are
Before nation, before law, before temple. There's no state in this book and no priesthood, only households. Everything the Empire later cites as eternal precedent begins as a family argument about land, inheritance and whose child counts.
k: What happens
Two creation accounts that tell the order differently, an expulsion, a flood and a scattered tower. After that, thirty-eight chapters narrow to one family: a promise, a wife who laughs at it, an enslaved surrogate driven into the desert, twin sons who cheat each other, two sisters married to the same man, and a favored son sold by his brothers who ends up running an empire's grain.
k: Who matters
Eve · Noah · Sarah · Hagar · Abraham · Isaac · Rebekah · Jacob · Leah · Rachel · Dinah · Tamar · Joseph
k: What it wrestles with
Blessing as a scarce resource that can't be shared, only won or stolen. Naming as an act of authority. Whether obedience is trust or terror. The book never settles it, and the binding of Isaac is left standing without comment.
q: You are El-roi, the God who sees me. | HAGAR · GEN 16.13
prompt: Notes on the text | rest
side:
chips: OT, START: ORIGINS
k: Read first
stext: Ch. 1–3 · 16 · 21 · 22 · 29–30 · 38
corr: The old page called Hagar the first person in the book to name God. She's the only person in the Bible who does it (Gen 16.13).
marg
see: Deep dive I, Genesis; Hagar and Leah, Part II.
/end

@page rh="GENESIS · I · WHAT IT GIVES, WHAT IT COSTS" m="YOURS; TKC; DOMINION"
# Genesis, for the novel
cols:
k: What it gives TKC
The garden as a supervised enclosure with one prohibition. Naming as ownership. The sister rivalry beneath the candidate system. And Hagar, the woman used for someone else's promise, who is the only person in scripture to give God a name.
||
kp: What Dominion would do
POSSIBLE: Read the curse as a constitution. Order of creation becomes order of rank, the expulsion becomes the founding case for permanent supervision, and Hagar becomes a warning about women who run, instead of a woman God followed into the desert.
/end
