@essay rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · OLD TESTAMENT · HISTORY · XI–XII"
# 1 and 2 Kings
small: forty-seven chapters · Books xi–xii of lxvi
k: Where we are
At the height of the state and all the way down. The book holds the temple's construction and its destruction. INFERENCE: In its final form it was finished in exile, by people explaining how they lost everything. Its last scene is a captive king in Babylon.
k: What happens
Solomon builds the temple with conscripted labor, and after his death the kingdom splits over that grievance. A catalogue of kings follows, each rated in a sentence, broken up by prophets who confront them. Elijah takes on a royal couple who seize a vineyard by judicial murder. A forgotten scroll is found in the temple and sets off a purge. Then Assyria, Babylon and the temple burned.
k: Who matters
Solomon · the queen of Sheba · Elijah · Ahab · Jezebel · Naboth · Elisha · Athaliah · Huldah · Josiah · Nebuchadnezzar
k: What it wrestles with
Why the state fell, answered theologically and not militarily. The kings were unfaithful. It's a convenient explanation and a dangerous one, because every disaster becomes somebody's insufficient devotion.
q: Have you killed, and also taken possession? | ELIJAH TO AHAB · 1 KGS 21.19
prompt: Notes on the text | rest
side:
chips: OT, START: EMPIRE
k: Read first
stext: 1 Kgs 3 · 5 · 12 · 18 · 21 · 2 Kgs 11 · 22–23 · 25
small: The biblical prophet Huldah has no connection to the Prelate. The Prelate's working name is Aurelia (POSSIBLE, FR 33).
marg
see: Jezebel and Huldah, Part II; Empires of the Bible, Part IV.
/end

@page rh="1 AND 2 KINGS · XI–XII · WHAT IT GIVES, WHAT IT COSTS" m="YOURS; TKC; DOMINION"
# 1 and 2 Kings, for the novel
cols:
k: What it gives TKC
Naboth's vineyard, which shows how a state takes what it wants and keeps the paperwork clean. Plus Huldah, the prophet who authenticates a found scroll for a king, and Athaliah, a queen whose reign was written up as usurpation.
||
kp: What Dominion would do
POSSIBLE: Preach the temple and the reforming king and skip the forced labor that built it. Jezebel becomes the standing image of a woman with political power, which is why her name survives as an insult and Naboth's doesn't.
/end
