@page rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · OLD TESTAMENT · THE OFFICIAL RECORD · XIII–XVI" m="1 CHR 1–9; 1 CHR 3.5; EZRA 9–10; NEH 5; JAPHET, I & II CHRONICLES"
# Chronicles · Ezra · Nehemiah
lede: These three share a page because they do one job. They're the authorized version, written after the exile by and for the restored establishment, retelling the national story with its failures managed. Here's what state-sponsored scripture looks like from inside.
rec:
1 and 2 Chronicles · sixty-five chapters | The same monarchy retold with the scandals removed. David's affair and Uriah's murder are gone, and Bathsheba stays only as a name in a list of Solomon's mother. Absalom's rebellion is gone. Nine chapters of genealogy open the book, because lineage is the argument: this priesthood, this line, this temple, legitimately. Where Kings explains a catastrophe, Chronicles builds a credential. **For TKC:** Dominion's own historiography. Two accounts of the same reign, one of them cleaned, and a character who finds both.
Ezra · ten chapters | A return permitted under imperial license, a rebuilt temple and a purity crisis in which the community is pressed to send away its foreign wives and their children. One of the coldest passages in scripture, seven books after Ruth, which says the opposite. **For TKC:** ethnic purity legislated as obedience, with the paperwork of restoration.
Nehemiah · thirteen chapters | A cupbearer to a foreign king gets leave to rebuild his city's walls, and it reads like a project memoir: opposition, logistics, night inspections, armed builders. In chapter 5 the poor are pledging their children against debt, and he makes the wealthy give back what they took. **For TKC:** walls, gates, registers and a reformer aimed at his own class.
cols:
kp: What Dominion would do with all three
POSSIBLE: Make Chronicles the standard history and let Kings go quietly out of print. Cite Ezra whenever purity needs enforcing and Nehemiah whenever a wall needs building, and never read Nehemiah 5 aloud.
||
contested: Ezra's divorces
Whether the mass divorce was survival for a tiny community or ethnic exclusion with a liturgy is argued in good faith both ways. The Empire has picked one.
/end
/end
corr: The old page said Bathsheba is gone from Chronicles. Her story is gone. Her name stays, as "Bath-shua" in 1 Chr 3.5. The old page also put Ezra four books from Ruth. It's seven.
prompt: Questions this raises about TKC | fill

@essay rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · OLD TESTAMENT · HISTORY · XVII"
# Esther
small: ten chapters · Book xvii of lxvi
k: Where we are
Inside the palace of a foreign empire, entirely. There's no temple, no prophet and, in the Hebrew text, no mention of God at all. It's the most useful book here for writing a woman surviving a court.
k: What happens
A queen refuses to be displayed and is removed. A search across the empire gathers young women into the palace for a year of beauty treatments. An orphan, told by her cousin to hide her people, becomes queen. A minister engineers a genocide. She risks an unsummoned approach, works the king through two banquets, and the minister hangs on the gallows he built. The king's decree can't be revoked, so a second decree lets her people defend themselves.
k: Who matters
Vashti · Esther · Mordecai · Haman · the king, whom everyone manipulates
k: What it wrestles with
How to act with no power, no God on the page and no guarantee. The book's ethics are improvised, concealment, timing, flattery and hospitality as strategy, and it never once says whether that's admirable.
q: If I perish, I perish. | ESTH 4.16
prompt: Notes on the text | rest
side:
chips: OT, START: CHARACTER
k: Read first
stext: Ch. 1 · 2 · 4 · 7 · 8. Read chapter 1 twice.
k: Echoes
stext: Eden · Celeste · Lyris
marg
see: Esther and Vashti, Part II.
/end

@page rh="ESTHER · XVII · WHAT IT GIVES, WHAT IT COSTS" m="YOURS; TKC; DOMINION"
# Esther, for the novel
cols:
k: What it gives TKC
The candidate system, nearly wholesale: a state selection, a year of preparation, a girl told to hide what she is. And Vashti, who says no in chapter one and is erased, which makes the whole plot possible.
||
kp: What Dominion would do
POSSIBLE: Canonize her as the patient wife, proof that a woman's power is beauty, obedience and correct timing. Vashti is preached as the warning, and the year of preparation is presented as honor and never as processing.
/end
prompt: Questions this raises about TKC | 8
