@page rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · OLD TESTAMENT · THE THREE ARGUMENTS · XX–XXII" m="PROV 8; PROV 31.10–31; ECCL 1–3; ECCL 9.7–10; SONG 8.6; MISHNAH YADAYIM 3.5"
# Proverbs · Ecclesiastes · Song of Songs
lede: Three books read against each other. Life is orderly. Life is vapor. And the third, which ignores both and looks at a body.
rec:
Proverbs · thirty-one chapters | Instruction for young men entering public life: work hard, hold your tongue, avoid the wrong woman. Wisdom herself is a woman who shouts in the streets and was present at creation (Prov 8), the boldest female figure in the Old Testament and rarely preached as one. It closes with a formidable woman running an estate, usually quoted at wives as a domestic standard. **For TKC:** the exact register of Dominion's advice literature, and the awkward fact that its own ideal woman is a businesswoman.
Ecclesiastes · twelve chapters | A man with unlimited resources tests every route to meaning and reports that all of it is vapor, that the wise and the fool meet the same end and that nobody will remember you. Its counsel is small. Eat your bread, love someone, the day is short. **For TKC:** the voice of someone inside the palace who has stopped believing the palace means anything. Devastating in a courtier's mouth.
Song of Songs · eight chapters | Erotic poetry, unembarrassed, with no mention of marriage, children or duty, and God named at most once, in a disputed word in 8.6. The woman speaks most of the lines, takes the initiative and is never rebuked for it. Centuries of allegorical reading exist mainly to make it respectable. **For TKC:** desire with scriptural standing, and a woman's voice as the loudest in the book. The Empire's most awkward inheritance.
cols:
kp: What Dominion would do with all three
POSSIBLE: Teach Proverbs as law, allegorize the Song until the bodies disappear, and leave Ecclesiastes out of the provincial edition.
||
contested: What the Song is
Whether it's human love poetry or an allegory of divine love has been argued for two thousand years. Which one Dominion teaches decides what the characters are allowed to want.
/end
/end
prompt: Questions this raises about TKC | fill

@essay rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · THE PROPHETS · BEFORE YOU READ ANY OF THEM"
# How prophecy works
drop: A prophet in this collection is less a fortune teller than someone who stands in public and tells a government what it's doing, in the name of a God the government also claims. Most of the job is that, and it explains why so many of them are run out of town, imprisoned or killed. Very little in these seventeen books is about the distant future. Nearly all of it is about the harvest, the courts, the treasury, the treaty and who's being crushed this season.
The form is consistent. There's an accusation naming a specific injustice, a verdict, and very often a reversal, the promise that something will be rebuilt after the collapse. Empires like the third part and get nervous about the first. A state religion teaches the prophets as comfort literature and hopes nobody reads the accusations closely enough to notice they're itemized: unjust weights, seized land, bribed judges, unpaid workers, widows without recourse and refugees turned away.
Two more things. The prophets attack worship itself, viciously, when it sits alongside exploitation. The festivals are hated, the offerings refused and the singing called noise. It's some of the most anti-clerical literature in existence, and it's in the canon. False prophecy is a live theme too, and the test isn't only accuracy. INTERPRETATION: The false prophet in these books is usually the one who tells power what it wants to hear (Jer 23, 28).
q: I hate, I despise your festivals… Take away from me the noise of your songs… But let justice roll down like waters. | AMOS 5.21–24
empire: What Dominion does with them
POSSIBLE: Reads the consolation passages at state occasions and treats the accusations as history, a complaint against ancient kings long settled. The office of prophet is folded into the priesthood, so anyone claiming it independently is unlicensed by definition.
/end
side:
chips: OT, PROPHETS
k: The seventeen
stext: Isaiah · Jeremiah · Lamentations · Ezekiel · Daniel · and the Twelve, Hosea through Malachi
k: What this gives TKC
stext: A tradition of sanctioned public speech against the state, sitting inside the Empire's own scripture. A dissident doesn't need a new ideology. She needs a lectionary and the nerve to read the unabridged version aloud.
marg
see: Focused studies F3, Ezekiel, and F7, the Twelve.
/end

