@page rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · THE PROPHETS · MAJOR · XXIV–XXV" m="JER 20; JER 26; JER 32; JER 36; JER 38; LAM 1–5; LAM 3.22–23"
# Jeremiah · Lamentations
lede: The collapse, and the sound a city makes afterward.
note: Siege famine and the deaths of children, Lamentations 2 and 4.
rec:
Jeremiah · fifty-two chapters | Forty years of telling a capital city it's going to fall, and being hated for it. He's beaten and put in the stocks (ch. 20), put on trial for his life (ch. 26), lowered into a muddy cistern (ch. 38) and accused of weakening the soldiers' morale, all by his own government, while the official prophets promise the city can't be taken. He's right. It falls. The king cuts his scroll apart column by column as it's read and burns it, so he dictates it again (ch. 36). His complaints to God are bitter enough to embarrass. He accuses God of deceiving him and curses the day he was born. In the middle of the siege he buys a field, on the record, as a bet there'll be a country again (ch. 32). **For TKC:** the licensed prophets against the one tried for treason. Also the burned scroll, and the fact that he simply wrote it again.
Lamentations · five chapters | Five poems over the ruins of the city. The first four are alphabetical acrostics, grief given the strictest possible structure because structure is all that's left, and the fifth keeps the twenty-two lines without the alphabet. It describes famine, mothers and children and the silence of God without excusing any of it, and it doesn't resolve. One passage in the middle says mercy is new every morning, and the book doesn't pretend that settles anything. **For TKC:** the register for the worst chapter. Grief as a disciplined public form, and a text the Empire can't ban because it's scripture and can't use because it blames the leadership.
cols:
kp: What Dominion would do
POSSIBLE: Teach Jeremiah as a warning about national sin, never about state censorship, and read only the mercy verse from Lamentations, on one fixed day of the year.
||
contested: Punishment or geography
Whether the fall was punishment or what happens to small states between empires. Jeremiah says the first. The events also support the second.
/end
/end
prompt: Questions this raises about TKC | fill

@page rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · THE PROPHETS · IN EXILE · XXVI–XXVII" m="EZEK 4–5; EZEK 10–11; EZEK 18; EZEK 24; EZEK 34; EZEK 37; DAN 1–6; DAN 7–12; 1 MACC 1"
# Ezekiel · Daniel
lede: Both written from inside somebody else's empire.
rec:
Ezekiel · forty-eight chapters | A deported priest with no temple to serve, receiving visions of extravagant strangeness: wheels full of eyes, the glory of God rising out of the temple and leaving the city eastward (ch. 10–11), a valley of dry bones standing up as an army (ch. 37). He performs his messages more than he speaks them, lying on one side for months, baking bread over dung, shaving his head. When his wife dies he's told not to mourn in public, as a sign. His indictment of the shepherds of Israel (ch. 34) is one of the sharpest attacks on a ruling class in the collection. You fed yourselves, you clothed yourselves with the wool, and you didn't seek the lost. He also insists a child doesn't carry a parent's guilt (ch. 18), which cuts straight against collective punishment. **For TKC:** INTERPRETATION: the mobile God. If the glory can leave the temple and appear to the deported by a foreign river, no state can hold it by holding the building.
Daniel · twelve chapters | Half court stories, half apocalypse, and the whole book is about keeping your integrity inside a regime that owns your career. Young captives are trained for imperial service and renamed. They negotiate over food, refuse to bow to a statue, survive a furnace and read the dreams the empire's own experts can't. A king goes mad, and another sees writing appear on his banquet wall and is dead that night. The visions in the second half show successive empires as beasts. INFERENCE: Most scholars date them to the persecution under Antiochus IV around 167 BCE, long after the story's setting, which is how coded resistance literature works. **For TKC:** the manual for a character inside the Ministry. Serve competently, refuse at one precise point, and let the refusal be public.
cols:
kp: What Dominion would do
POSSIBLE: Teach Daniel as a story about personal purity, diet, prayer discipline and courage under trial, and never as a story about refusing to bow to a state image.
||
k: What these give TKC
Ezekiel's shepherds as an indictment of the clergy, and his rule that guilt isn't inherited, the passage that takes collective punishment apart.
/end
prompt: Questions this raises about TKC | fill

@page rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · THE PROPHETS · THE TWELVE · XXVIII–XXXII" m="HOS 1–3; JOEL 2.28–29; AMOS 2.6; AMOS 7.10–15; OBAD 11–14; JONAH 4"
# The Twelve
lede: Hosea through Jonah. Short, furious and mostly about money. The Hebrew Bible counts all twelve as a single book.
tabrl:
| Book | What it is | What it gives TKC |
| Hosea · 14 ch. | A prophet's marriage staged as national metaphor. Infidelity, and a God who keeps taking the nation back. Tender and disturbing in equal measure. | Covenant as marriage, the theology behind Dominion's whole language of wifely duty. |
| Joel · 3 ch. | A locust plague read as the day of the Lord, ending with the spirit poured out on everyone, sons and daughters, old and young, male and female slaves. | Prophecy for everyone. If daughters prophesy, the Ministry's monopoly is a scriptural error. |
| Amos · 9 ch. | A herdsman from Judah goes north and denounces a prosperous kingdom for selling the needy for a pair of sandals. The royal priest tells him to go home. | Some of the most quotable indictment of a comfortable religious state in existence. |
| Obadiah · 1 ch. | One chapter aimed at a neighboring nation for standing by and profiting while a city was sacked. | Complicity as its own crime. The bystander province. |
| Jonah · 4 ch. | A satire. A prophet runs from an order to preach to the enemy capital, is swallowed, obeys, succeeds, and sulks because God spared them. | Mercy for the enemy, and a believer who finds it unbearable. |
prompt: Notes | fill

@page rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · THE PROPHETS · THE TWELVE · XXXIII–XXXIX" m="MIC 3; MIC 6.8; HAB 1–3; ZEPH 1.8; HAG 1.4; ZECH 3; ZECH 9.9; MAL 1–2; MAL 4.5–6"
# The Twelve, continued
lede: Micah through Malachi. The Old Testament in Christian order ends by auditing its own clergy.
tabrl:
| Book | What it is | What it gives TKC |
| Micah · 7 ch. | Judgment on rulers who eat their own people, and the collection's clearest summary of the whole demand. Do justice, love kindness, walk humbly. | Three phrases that fit on a wall, and indict the wall's owner. |
| Nahum · 3 ch. | The fall of an imperial capital, described with something close to relish. | What the provinces sing when the center burns. |
| Habakkuk · 3 ch. | A prophet argues with God about why injustice is permitted, gets an unsatisfying answer and decides to trust anyway. | Faith as an argument you lose and stay in. |
| Zephaniah · 3 ch. | A total judgment on the ruling class, with the officials and the king's sons named first and a humble remnant left over. | The purge sermon the Ministry would love, aimed at itself. |
| Haggai · 2 ch. | Rebuild the temple, said to people living in paneled houses while the site lies in ruins. | State religion as a construction project. |
| Zechariah · 14 ch. | Night visions, a high priest re-robed in a heavenly courtroom, and a king who comes on a donkey and not a warhorse. | Ceremony as legitimation, and power that refuses the cavalry. |
| Malachi · 4 ch. | An audit of the priesthood: blemished offerings, withheld tithes, men abandoning the wives of their youth. Its closing lines promise Elijah before the day of the Lord. | Corrupt priests, on the record, in the Empire's own book. |
corr: The old page said the last word of the Old Testament is an accusation against clergy. The audit is Malachi 1–2. The last verses (4.5–6) promise Elijah and warn of a curse. In the Hebrew order, the Bible ends with Cyrus's decree in 2 Chronicles.
prompt: Which of the Twelve is banned in Dominion, and why | fill

@essay rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · NEW TESTAMENT · THE GOSPELS · XL–XLIII"
# Four accounts
drop: There are four of these, and they don't match, which is the most useful thing about them. The differences are four communities, decades apart, deciding what mattered about one life. Most people who quote the gospels are quoting a blend of all four that exists in no manuscript, and untangling the blend is the same act of attention your characters will have to perform on Dominion's teaching.
rec:
Mark · earliest, shortest | Urgent and bleak. No birth story. The disciples never quite understand him, his family thinks he's out of his mind, and he keeps telling people to say nothing. In the earliest manuscripts it ends at 16.8, with women running from an empty tomb, afraid, telling no one. Read this one first.
Matthew · the teacher | Argues that this is the fulfillment of the law, so it's built in five great blocks of teaching and cites scripture constantly. Its genealogy includes four women besides Mary, Tamar, Rahab, Ruth and "the wife of Uriah," on purpose. Holds the Sermon on the Mount, the Lord's Prayer and a whole chapter denouncing religious authorities by title (ch. 23).
Luke · the reversal | Keeps women, foreigners and the poor in the frame. It opens with a young woman singing that the powerful will be pulled from their thrones and the hungry filled, and it's the only gospel that names women paying for the whole operation (8.1–3). Its parables are the most economically pointed in scripture.
John · theology at altitude | Long discourses, cosmic language and seven signs. It gives one of the longest conversations in the gospels to a foreign woman at a well, has Martha make the central confession of faith (11.27), and makes Mary Magdalene the first person the risen Jesus speaks to, sent to tell the men.
side:
chips: NT, GOSPELS
k: What this gives TKC
stext: A character who finds two accounts that disagree. The crisis isn't faith. It's about who did the editing, which is far more dangerous.
corr: The old page called Mary a pregnant teenager. Luke never gives her age. It's an inference from ancient marriage customs.
marg
see: The Gospel Grid, Part III.
/end

@page rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · NEW TESTAMENT · THE GOSPELS · YOUR PAGE" m="YOURS; GOSPELS"
