@essay rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · NEW TESTAMENT · THE MOVEMENT · XLIV"
# Acts
small: twenty-eight chapters · Book xliv of lxvi
k: Where we are
An unlicensed movement spreading across the Roman world with no buildings, no budget and no legal standing, meeting mostly in homes, borrowed rooms and courtrooms.
k: What happens
A spirit arrives and everyone hears in their own language. The first community pools its property. A couple who lie about money fall dead. A persecutor is converted on a road. Then the great fight over whether outsiders have to adopt the law to belong, settled by a meeting in Jerusalem (ch. 15). Again and again the meeting place is a woman's house, Mary's in Jerusalem (12.12) and Lydia's in Philippi (16.15, 40).
k: Who matters
Peter · Stephen · Philip · Paul · Tabitha · Lydia · Priscilla · the Ethiopian official · Gamaliel
k: What it wrestles with
Who's allowed in, and who decides. Every widening of the boundary is resisted first by insiders and accepted after somebody tells a story about what they saw.
q: All who believed were together and had all things in common. | ACTS 2.44
prompt: Notes on the text | rest
side:
chips: NT, START: RESISTANCE
k: Read first
stext: Ch. 2 · 4–5 · 8 · 12 · 15 · 16
marg
see: Lydia, Part II; Religious institutions, Part IV.
/end

@page rh="ACTS · XLIV · WHAT IT GIVES, WHAT IT COSTS" m="YOURS; TKC; DOMINION"
# Acts, for the novel
