@essay rh="THE SIXTY-SIX BOOKS · NEW TESTAMENT · APOCALYPTIC · LXVI"
# Revelation
small: twenty-two chapters · Book lxvi of lxvi
k: Where we are
On the island of Patmos, "because of the word of God" (1.9), writing to seven congregations under pressure from an empire whose emperors were worshiped as gods. INFERENCE: Tradition says he was exiled there. Either way it's dissident literature, written in code.
k: What happens
Seven letters assessing seven churches, several of them harshly. Then visions: a slaughtered lamb on the throne, seals and trumpets and bowls, a beast whose authority comes from a dragon, a mark required to buy and sell, and a gorgeously dressed city called a prostitute whose fall is mourned by merchants because their trade is ruined. It ends with that city replaced by a new one whose gates never close.
k: Who matters
John of Patmos · the lamb · the dragon and the beast · the woman clothed with the sun · the merchants, who are the ones who weep
k: What it wrestles with
Whether empires end. Its answer is that they do, that their economy gives them away, and that the throne belongs to something that was killed and not to something that kills.
q: Come out of her, my people. | REV 18.4
prompt: Notes on the text | rest
side:
chips: NT, START: DOMINION
k: Read first
stext: Ch. 2–3 · 5 · 12–13 · 17–18 · 21
marg
see: The shape of the New Testament, Revelation, module v.
/end

@page rh="REVELATION · LXVI · WHAT IT GIVES, WHAT IT COSTS" m="YOURS; TKC; DOMINION"
# Revelation, for the novel
cols:
k: What it gives TKC
A whole visual and rhetorical vocabulary: marks, seals, thrones, beasts, a bride, a prostitute, gates that never close. And the political core, an empire's fall told through the grief of its merchants.
||
kp: What Dominion would do
POSSIBLE: Move the whole thing to the distant future and appoint itself the interpreter. If the beast is always still coming, it can never be the government in the room.
/end
